        "end_line": 312,
        "quote": "\t@SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n\tprotected <T> @Nullable T convertValueIfNecessary(Object value, @Nullable Class<T> targetType) {\n\t\tif (targetType == null) {\n\t\t\treturn (T) value;\n\t\t}\n\t\tConversionService conversionServiceToUse = this.conversionService;\n\t\tif (conversionServiceToUse == null) {\n\t\t\t// Avoid initialization of shared DefaultConversionService if\n\t\t\t// no standard type conversion is needed in the first place...\n\t\t\tif (ClassUtils.isAssignableValue(targetType, value)) {\n\t\t\t\treturn (T) value;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconversionServiceToUse = DefaultConversionService.getSharedInstance();\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn conversionServiceToUse.convert(value, targetType);\n\t}",
        "explanation": "The value entering this method is the post-placeholder value. A null target type returns that same object, and, while no resolver-specific service exists, an already assignable value is likewise retained without conversion. Otherwise the shared DefaultConversionService is used. setConversionService rejects null and stores the supplied ConfigurableConversionService; once present, it is used even for assignable values. getConversionService lazily creates, stores, and thereafter returns an independent DefaultConversionService rather than the shared instance, so merely calling it establishes the resolver-specific service used by later conversions. The shared fallback is not stored in the resolver. A conversion result, including null, is returned directly and is not cached. Conversion exceptions are not caught, so they abort lookup and no lower source is considered."
